    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: Data input\nguide-section: \"Results\"\nbread-crumbs: false\n---\n\n\n\nWhittaker accepts data as a Python dictionary mapping column names to 1-D NumPy arrays. This is the\nsimplest and most portable data format. It works with any data source and avoids coupling the\nlibrary to a specific DataFrame backend.\n\n## The data dictionary\n\nEvery `fit()` and `predict()` call expects a dictionary where keys are column names (strings) and\nvalues are 1-D NumPy arrays of the same length.\n\n::: {#c96a4538 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport numpy as np\nimport whittaker as wk\n\n# The data dictionary: one key per column, one array per key\ndata = {\n    \"x1\": np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 100),\n    \"x2\": np.random.default_rng(42).uniform(0, 1, 100),\n    \"y\": np.sin(np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 100)) + np.random.default_rng(42).normal(0, 0.3, 100),\n}\n\n# Fit a model (the formula references keys from the dictionary)\nmodel = wk.GAM(\"y ~ s(x1) + s(x2)\")\nmodel.fit(data, method=\"REML\")\n\nprint(f\"Fitted with {len(data['y'])} observations\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\nFitted with 100 observations\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nEvery column referenced in the formula must be present in the dictionary. Extra columns are ignored.\n\n## Converting from DataFrames\n\nIf your data is in a Polars or Pandas DataFrame, convert it to a dictionary before passing it to\nWhittaker.\n\n### From Polars\n\n::: {#2edbd097 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport polars as pl\n\n# Create a Polars DataFrame\ndf_pl = pl.DataFrame({\n    \"x\": np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 100),\n    \"y\": np.sin(np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 100)) + np.random.default_rng(42).normal(0, 0.3, 100),\n})\n\n# Convert to a dict of NumPy arrays\ndata_from_polars = {col: df_pl[col].to_numpy() for col in df_pl.columns}\n\n# Fit the model\nmodel = wk.GAM(\"y ~ s(x)\")\nmodel.fit(data_from_polars, method=\"REML\")\nprint(f\"Fitted from Polars: EDF = {model.edf_total:.1f}\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\nFitted from Polars: EDF = 7.9\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### From Pandas\n\n::: {#8327d887 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Create a Pandas DataFrame\ndf_pd = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"x\": np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 100),\n    \"y\": np.sin(np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 100)) + np.random.default_rng(42).normal(0, 0.3, 100),\n})\n\n# Convert to a dict of NumPy arrays\ndata_from_pandas = {col: df_pd[col].to_numpy() for col in df_pd.columns}\n\n# Fit the model\nmodel = wk.GAM(\"y ~ s(x)\")\nmodel.fit(data_from_pandas, method=\"REML\")\nprint(f\"Fitted from Pandas: EDF = {model.edf_total:.1f}\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\nFitted from Pandas: EDF = 7.9\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {.callout-tip}\n## Zero-copy conversion\n\nFor Polars DataFrames backed by Arrow arrays, `to_numpy()` is often zero-copy, so no data is\nduplicated. For Pandas with NumPy-backed columns, `to_numpy()` returns a view of the underlying\narray. This means conversion is essentially free for typical numerical data.\n:::\n\n## Prediction data\n\nThe `predict()` method accepts the same dictionary format. You only need to include the covariate\ncolumns; the response column is not required for prediction.\n\n::: {#1259c765 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n# Predict on new data (only covariate columns are needed)\nnew_data = {\"x\": np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 50)}\n\npreds = model.predict(new_data)\nprint(f\"Predictions shape: {preds.values.shape}\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}\n```\nPredictions shape: (50,)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#f6802391 .cell execution_count=6}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport altair as alt\n\nx_plot = np.linspace(0, 2 * np.pi, 100)\npred_plot = model.predict({\"x\": x_plot}, se=True)\n\nplot_data = [\n    {\"x\": float(x_plot[i]), \"fit\": float(pred_plot.values[i]),\n     \"lower\": float(pred_plot.values[i] - 1.96 * pred_plot.se[i]),\n     \"upper\": float(pred_plot.values[i] + 1.96 * pred_plot.se[i])}\n    for i in range(len(x_plot))\n]\n\npts = alt.Chart(\n    {\"values\": [{\"x\": float(data_from_pandas[\"x\"][i]), \"y\": float(data_from_pandas[\"y\"][i])}\n     for i in range(len(data_from_pandas[\"y\"]))]}\n).mark_circle(size=15, opacity=0.3, color=\"steelblue\").encode(\n    x=alt.X(\"x:Q\", title=\"x\"), y=alt.Y(\"y:Q\", title=\"y\"),\n)\n\nband = alt.Chart({\"values\": plot_data}).mark_area(\n    opacity=0.2, color=\"firebrick\"\n).encode(x=\"x:Q\", y=\"lower:Q\", y2=\"upper:Q\")\n\nline = alt.Chart({\"values\": plot_data}).mark_line(\n    color=\"firebrick\", strokeWidth=2\n).encode(x=\"x:Q\", y=\"fit:Q\")\n\n(band + pts + line).properties(\n    width=500, height=300, title=\"Predictions from the fitted GAM\"\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=6}\n```{=html}\n\n<style>\n  #altair-viz-83fcc64a95f841de8f6f06f714e8ead7.vega-embed {\n    width: 100%;\n    display: flex;\n  }\n\n  #altair-viz-83fcc64a95f841de8f6f06f714e8ead7.vega-embed details,\n  #altair-viz-83fcc64a95f841de8f6f06f714e8ead7.vega-embed details summary {\n    position: relative;\n  }\n</style>\n<div id=\"altair-viz-83fcc64a95f841de8f6f06f714e8ead7\"></div>\n<script type=\"text/javascript\">\n  var VEGA_DEBUG = (typeof VEGA_DEBUG == \"undefined\") ? {} : VEGA_DEBUG;\n  (function(spec, embedOpt){\n    let outputDiv = document.currentScript.previousElementSibling;\n    if (outputDiv.id !== \"altair-viz-83fcc64a95f841de8f6f06f714e8ead7\") {\n      outputDiv = document.getElementById(\"altair-viz-83fcc64a95f841de8f6f06f714e8ead7\");\n    }\n\n    const paths = {\n      \"vega\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/vega@6?noext\",\n      \"vega-lib\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/vega-lib?noext\",\n      \"vega-lite\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/vega-lite@6.4.1?noext\",\n      \"vega-embed\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/vega-embed@7?noext\",\n    };\n\n    function maybe